//!
//! # Higher Half Direct Map
//!
//! Access to any physical address through Limine's direct map.
//!
//! ## Overview
//!
//! Limine maps all physical memory at a fixed virtual offset, so physical
//! address `p` is readable at virtual address `offset + p`:
//!
//! ```text
//!   physical                         virtual
//!   0x0000_0000_0000_0000  ───────►  offset + 0x0
//!   0x0000_0000_1234_5000  ───────►  offset + 0x1234_5000
//!   ...                              (offset is typically 0xffff800000000000)
//! ```
//!
//! The kernel's own page tables (`vmm.ign`) rebuild the same mapping with the
//! same offset, so pointers made here stay valid across the CR3 switch in
//! `Mm::Vmm::initialize`. The kernel uses it to edit page tables, read ACPI
//! tables, touch memory-mapped device registers and hand out physical frames
//! as ordinary pointers. See concept doc 09.

import _ from "./limine";

/// Conversions between physical addresses and direct-map pointers.
namespace Boot::Hhdm {
  /// The virtual address of physical address zero. Panics when Limine provided
  /// no direct map; the answer is read from the response on every call.
  function offset(): u64 {
    let response: *mut Boot::Limine::HhdmResponse = Boot::Limine::hhdm();

    if (response == null) {
      @panic("Limine provided no higher half direct map");
    }

    return (*response).offset;
  }

  /// A pointer to physical address `physical`. The address must lie in a
  /// region the direct map covers; this is not checked. The memory type of the
  /// mapping is write-back, so device registers need the special mappings
  /// `Vmm` provides instead.
  function toVirtual(physical: u64): *mut u8 {
    return @pointerFromInteger<*mut u8>(offset() + physical);
  }
}
